arrow{d}\; N(0,1). \] A practical consequence is that for large \(n\) we can use normal‐based confidence intervals and hypothesis tests for \(\mu\) even when the parent distribution of the data is unknown or skewed; the accuracy of this normal approximation improves as the sample size grows.
